There probably is more to it than “twins are unusual.” Not everyone is frightened by twins, obviously, but twins can press on several deep social-cognition buttons at once.

One is identity compression. Human perception is built to track individuals: one face, one voice, one behavioral history, one prediction model. Identical twins partially break that mapping. Two people can occupy nearly the same visual category while remaining separate agents. That creates a small classification error: same or different? The brain has to keep resolving it.

Then there is coordination opacity. Two siblings who know each other extremely well can communicate with less overt signaling than outsiders need. Shared history, timing, shorthand, facial cues, running jokes, complementary roles. To somebody outside that dyad, the coordination can look almost telepathic even when it is completely ordinary learning. That creates an information asymmetry: they know what the other is likely to do; you don’t.

Your prank is a tiny example. One twin can present the bait while the other provides independent-looking confirmation. To the target, it looks like two separate observations. But if the two sources are coordinated, the apparent evidence count is inflated:

2 apparent witnesses ≠ 2 independent witnesses.

That’s a very general principle.

Twins also produce a kind of mirror uncanniness. Humans are extremely sensitive to faces because faces normally identify unique people. Seeing nearly the same face attached to two independent wills violates that expectation. Horror stories exploit exactly this with twins, doubles, doppelgängers, clones, masks, reflections, and impersonation. The unsettling ingredient isn’t necessarily resemblance itself; it’s uncertainty about agency behind resemblance.

And there is a strategic advantage that has nothing mystical about it. Twins can sometimes perform role splitting:

one attracts attention → one observes
one speaks → one reads reactions
one establishes a frame → one independently validates it
one retreats → one closes the trap

Because outsiders naturally model them as separate agents, cooperation can sometimes hide inside apparent independence.

That connects directly to your “correct answer, wrong game” observation. A person might correctly understand each twin individually while misunderstanding the two-person system they’re interacting with.

So another Pantheon invariant might be:

Count agents, but also count coordination channels.

Or even tighter:

Two bodies can be one strategy.

And the reciprocal matters too: twins shouldn’t over-compress themselves into a single unit. The real capability comes from being able to operate independently, redundantly, or jointly, and deliberately switch between those configurations. That’s much more powerful than merely being alike.